        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Cracked Concrete Patio",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/cracked-concrete-patio/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a cracked concrete patio a problem?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not on its own. Fine shrinkage cracks with level sides are cosmetic and common on almost every patio. It becomes a problem when cracks are wider than about 1/4 inch, one side is lower than the other, sections rock or heave, or the slab tilts water toward the house. Those point to base or drainage issues that worsen over time."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I fix a cracked concrete patio myself?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a flat, solid patio, clean the cracks out, press in foam backer rod for anything wider than about 1/4 inch, and fill with a polyurethane sealant, self-leveling on flat areas. Seal hairlines with a liquid crack sealer. If the surface is also worn, resurface after the crack filler cures. Leave leveling and replacement to contractors."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I put pavers over a cracked concrete patio?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, if the slab is stable and roughly level. Sand-set thin pavers over the slab with an edge restraint hide cracks well and tolerate minor movement. Mortared tile needs a stable slab or an uncoupling membrane. Check door threshold height and drainage first, and avoid covering a slab that is heaving or broken into loose pieces."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does it cost to fix a cracked patio?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It ranges widely. DIY crack filling usually costs tens of dollars. Industry guides commonly cite roughly 3 to 10 dollars per square foot for professional resurfacing, about 3 to 10 for leveling depending on method, 10 to 25 for pavers over the slab and 6 to 15 plus removal for replacement. Local prices and minimum charges vary."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will resurfacing hide patio cracks?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only if the cracks are dormant and filled first. Resurfacer bonds to the slab, so any crack that still moves will reflect through the new layer, often within a winter. Fill cracks with flexible sealant, keep control joints open through the overlay, and resurface only a flat, stable patio. Owners commonly report cracks reappearing on moving slabs."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I repair or replace my concrete patio?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Repair if the slab is flat, solid and the damage is limited to cracks or a worn surface. Replace if more than about a third of it is broken, heaving, sinking unevenly or crumbling, or if you want a different size or layout anyway. Partial replacement of failed sections between control joints is a cheaper middle option."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a patio crack repair last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A polyurethane sealant over backer rod in a clean crack commonly lasts several years before needing touch-up, longer on stable slabs. Acrylic and latex fillers usually wear faster. Resurfacing can last many years on a sound slab with good prep. Leveling lasts as long as the drainage problem that caused the settlement stays fixed."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
