    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A cracked concrete patio has five possible fixes: fill the cracks, resurface the slab, cover it with pavers or tile, lift and level the sunken sections, or tear it out and pour new. They range from a weekend and fifty dollars to several thousand dollars and a demolition crew. The cracks themselves tell you which one fits. Fine shrinkage cracks on a flat slab are a filling job. Wide, offset cracks on a slab that rocks or holds water are not.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="read-cracks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What the Cracks Are Telling You
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shrinkage Cracks</h3>

          <p>
            Nearly every concrete patio cracks. Concrete shrinks as it cures and dries, and if the control joints were too far apart, too shallow or cut too late, the slab picks its own crack lines. Those shrinkage cracks are usually fine, under about 1/8 inch wide, and the two sides stay level with each other. They are cosmetic, and they are the easiest kind to live with or fix.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Settlement Cracks</h3>

          <p>
            Settlement cracks are different. They form when the soil under part of the slab compacts, washes out or was never compacted properly in the first place. The telltale sign is offset: one side of the crack sits lower than the other. Patios built over backfill next to a foundation are especially prone to this, because that soil keeps settling for years. Settlement cracks often widen over time, and the low side can tilt water back toward the house.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heave Cracks</h3>

          <p>
            Heave cracks come from the ground pushing up, usually frost in poorly drained soil, expansive clay, or tree roots. The slab lifts at one spot and cracks across the high point. Heave can be seasonal, rising in winter and settling back in spring, which means any rigid repair will fail. Look for cracks that open and close through the year, and for sections that rock when you step on them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Failed Slab</h3>

          <p>
            Then there is a slab that has simply failed: many cracks running in different directions, pieces broken loose, crumbling edges and a surface that has scaled away. That pattern usually means a thin pour, no reinforcement, a poor base, or years of freeze-thaw and deicers. At that point the question is not how to repair it but whether it is worth repairing at all.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
