        <div id="fill" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Option 1: Fill the Cracks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Filling is how to fix a cracked concrete patio when the slab is flat, solid and the cracks are the only problem. It is also the right first step before most of the other options. The goal is not to make the crack disappear, which rarely happens, but to keep water out of it. Water in a crack freezes, expands and slowly widens it, and it also washes out the base beneath the slab.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which Filler to Use</h3>

          <p>
            For hairline cracks, a thin liquid crack sealer in a squeeze bottle wicks in and seals them. For cracks from about 1/8 to 1/2 inch, use a polyurethane sealant, self-leveling on a flat patio or non-sag on a sloped one. Clean the crack out thoroughly with a screwdriver, a wire brush and a blower or shop vacuum. For cracks wider than about 1/4 inch, press foam backer rod in first so the sealant depth is roughly half the crack width.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cost and Timing</h3>

          <p>
            Patio crack repair done this way typically costs very little. A tube of polyurethane sealant and a roll of backer rod together usually run in the tens of dollars, and a patio with a few cracks takes an afternoon. Apply when the slab is dry and within the label temperature range, commonly 40 degrees Fahrenheit or warmer, and keep feet off it for a day. Our <a href="/best-concrete-crack-filler/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best concrete crack filler</a> guide ranks specific products by crack type.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Filled Cracks Look Like</h3>

          <p>
            Be realistic about appearance. Filled cracks remain visible as a gray line, and sand-textured sealants help but do not hide them. If the look bothers you, filling is a holding step before resurfacing or staining. If the patio is under furniture, a grill and a rug for most of the year, a clean, sealed crack is often all it needs. Our <a href="/concrete-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete crack repair</a> guide covers the full diagnosis and step-by-step process.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Leave Control Joints Flexible</h3>

          <p>
            Do not fill control joints rigidly. Those straight, intentional grooves are where the slab is supposed to crack, and they need to move. If you want to seal them against weeds and water, use the same flexible polyurethane sealant and backer rod, never a cement patch or epoxy. Filling a control joint with something rigid just moves the next crack somewhere you do not want it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="resurface" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Option 2: Resurface the Slab
          </h2>

          <p>
            Resurfacing puts a thin layer of polymer-modified cement over the whole patio, usually 1/8 to 1/2 inch thick. It is the right fix when the slab is sound and level but the surface looks rough: scaled from deicers, pitted, covered in popouts or stained beyond cleaning. It gives a uniform new surface, can be broom-finished for traction and accepts stain or sealer afterward.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fill Active Cracks First</h3>

          <p>
            Resurfacing does not fix active cracks. A crack that moves will reflect up through the new layer, usually within a winter. The right sequence is to fill the cracks with a flexible sealant first, let it cure, then resurface while keeping the control joints open by taping them off or recutting them. Owners commonly report that cracks show through anyway on slabs that move, so resurface a patio only if the cracks are dormant and the slab is flat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Resurfacing Costs</h3>

          <p>
            As a DIY job, materials for resurfacing usually run in the range of one to a few dollars per square foot depending on thickness and product, so a 200 square foot patio is often a few hundred dollars. Industry cost guides commonly list professional resurfacing in a range of roughly 3 to 10 dollars per square foot, with decorative stamped or stained overlays costing more. Our guide to the <a href="/best-concrete-resurfacer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best concrete resurfacers</a> covers the main bagged products.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Preparation Decides Whether It Lasts</h3>

          <p>
            Preparation decides whether resurfacing lasts. Pressure wash at around 3,000 psi to remove dirt, sealers and any weak surface layer. Repair spalls and holes deeper than the resurfacer allows with a patching mortar first. Dampen the slab to a saturated surface dry condition, mix in small batches and work in sections between control joints. Most products give about 20 minutes of working time in mild weather, less in heat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cover" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Option 3: Cover It With Pavers or Tile
          </h2>

          <p>
            Covering a cracked slab with pavers, flagstone or porcelain tile hides the cracks completely and changes the look of the patio. It works well when the slab is stable and roughly flat but cosmetically beyond saving, or when you wanted a different finish anyway. The old concrete becomes the base, which saves the demolition and base preparation that make up much of the cost of a new paver patio.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two Methods</h3>

          <p>
            There are two methods. Thin pavers, often about an inch thick, are laid on a thin sand bed over the slab with an edge restraint around the perimeter. Tile or stone is mortared directly to the concrete. Mortared finishes need a stable slab; any crack that moves will crack the tile or grout above it unless an uncoupling membrane is used. Sand-set pavers are more forgiving because each paver can move slightly on its own.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Height Is the Catch</h3>

          <p>
            Height is the catch. Adding 1 to 2 inches to the patio surface can bring it too close to a door threshold, create a step at the lawn edge or block drainage. Measure the threshold before committing. Most building guidance calls for a drop of several inches from the door sill to the patio surface, and a patio needs a slope of roughly 1/4 inch per foot away from the house.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Covering Costs</h3>

          <p>
            Pavers over concrete commonly cost from roughly 10 to 25 dollars per square foot installed, depending on the paver and region, which can approach the cost of a new paver patio on a fresh base. As a DIY project, materials for thin pavers are often a few dollars to around 10 dollars per square foot. Our comparison of <a href="/pavers-vs-concrete/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">pavers vs concrete</a> covers long-term costs and maintenance.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Not to Cover</h3>

          <p>
            Do not cover a slab that is heaving, sinking or broken into loose pieces. Pavers laid on a moving base will move with it, and mortared tile will crack along the same lines as the concrete. If the slab rocks, fix that first by leveling or by removing the bad sections, and only then use it as a base.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="level" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Option 4: Lift and Level a Sunken Patio
          </h2>

          <p>
            If the patio cracked because part of it sank, the fix is to raise it. Mudjacking pumps a cement-based slurry under the slab through holes about 1 to 2 inches across. Polyjacking, also called foam leveling, injects expanding polyurethane foam through holes around 5/8 inch. Both lift the low sections back toward level and fill the voids that caused the settlement in the first place.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Leveling Works</h3>

          <p>
            Leveling suits patios where sections have dropped but stayed largely intact, especially near the house where they are tilting water toward the foundation. It does not work well on slabs broken into many small pieces, which tend to lift unevenly and crack further. After leveling, the cracks still need sealing, and the drill holes are patched and remain faintly visible.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mudjacking vs Polyjacking Costs</h3>

          <p>
            Industry cost guides commonly list mudjacking in a range of roughly 3 to 6 dollars per square foot and polyjacking somewhat higher, often around 5 to 10 dollars per square foot, with minimum job charges for small areas. Foam is lighter, cures faster and uses smaller holes; slurry is cheaper. Our guides to <a href="/mudjacking-vs-polyjacking/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">mudjacking vs polyjacking</a> and <a href="/concrete-leveling-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete leveling cost</a> go into the trade-offs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fix the Water First</h3>

          <p>
            Leveling only lasts if the reason the soil washed out is fixed. A downspout emptying next to the patio, a grade that sends water under the slab, or a leaking hose bib will undermine it again. Extend downspouts at least several feet past the patio edge and seal the joint between the patio and the house with a flexible sealant so water stops running underneath.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Option 5: Tear Out and Replace
          </h2>

          <p>
            Replacement is the right call when the slab has failed structurally. That means many intersecting cracks with loose pieces, large sections that have heaved or sunk unevenly, widespread crumbling, or a patio so thin it breaks under normal use. It is also worth considering when you want to change the size, shape or height of the patio anyway, since any repair keeps the old layout.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do the Base Properly</h3>

          <p>
            A new patio fixes the cause, not just the symptom, but only if the base is done properly. That typically means removing the old slab, excavating soft or organic soil, installing several inches of compacted gravel, setting forms with a slope of about 1/4 inch per foot away from the house, and pouring at least 4 inches of concrete with control joints cut at intervals of spaced in feet at roughly two to three times the slab thickness in inches, so 8 to 12 feet for a 4 inch slab.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Replacement Costs</h3>

          <p>
            Costs vary widely by region, size and finish. Industry guides commonly put a plain broom-finished concrete patio in a range of roughly 6 to 15 dollars per square foot installed, with removal of the old slab adding a few dollars per square foot. Stamped and colored finishes cost substantially more. Our <a href="/concrete-patio-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete patio cost</a> guide breaks this down by size and finish.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Partial Replacement</h3>

          <p>
            Partial replacement is a middle path that contractors do not always mention. If one or two sections between control joints have failed and the rest of the patio is fine, those sections can be saw-cut out along the joints and repoured. The new concrete will not match in color for a while, but it costs a fraction of a full replacement and keeps the sound concrete in place.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="decide" class="scroll-mt-24">
