          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Simple Rule for Deciding</h3>

          <p>
            The simplest rule: if the slab is flat and solid, repair it. Fill the cracks, and resurface if the surface also looks bad. If it is solid but sunken, level it and then fill. If it is solid but you want a different look, cover it. If it is broken into loose pieces, heaving every winter, or crumbling across most of its area, replace it, or at least replace the failed sections.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE 30 PERCENT RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">When more than about a third of the patio needs serious work, price replacement.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Contractors commonly use a rough threshold like this. Below it, targeted repairs usually win. Above it, layered repairs can cost close to a new slab and still sit on the same weak base.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Protect the Result</h3>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, protect the result. Apply a penetrating sealer to reduce water uptake, use calcium magnesium acetate or another concrete-safe deicer rather than rock salt, and keep downspouts and grading sending water away from the slab. Our guides on <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete sealers</a> and <a href="/concrete-repair-products/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete repair products</a> cover what to buy, and our <a href="/concrete-repair-kit/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete repair kit</a> guide explains when a boxed kit is worth it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a cracked concrete patio a problem?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not on its own. Fine shrinkage cracks with level sides are cosmetic and common on almost every patio. It becomes a problem when cracks are wider than about 1/4 inch, one side is lower than the other, sections rock or heave, or the slab tilts water toward the house. Those point to base or drainage issues that worsen over time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I fix a cracked concrete patio myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a flat, solid patio, clean the cracks out, press in foam backer rod for anything wider than about 1/4 inch, and fill with a polyurethane sealant, self-leveling on flat areas. Seal hairlines with a liquid crack sealer. If the surface is also worn, resurface after the crack filler cures. Leave leveling and replacement to contractors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put pavers over a cracked concrete patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if the slab is stable and roughly level. Sand-set thin pavers over the slab with an edge restraint hide cracks well and tolerate minor movement. Mortared tile needs a stable slab or an uncoupling membrane. Check door threshold height and drainage first, and avoid covering a slab that is heaving or broken into loose pieces.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to fix a cracked patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It ranges widely. DIY crack filling usually costs tens of dollars. Industry guides commonly cite roughly 3 to 10 dollars per square foot for professional resurfacing, about 3 to 10 for leveling depending on method, 10 to 25 for pavers over the slab and 6 to 15 plus removal for replacement. Local prices and minimum charges vary.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will resurfacing hide patio cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the cracks are dormant and filled first. Resurfacer bonds to the slab, so any crack that still moves will reflect through the new layer, often within a winter. Fill cracks with flexible sealant, keep control joints open through the overlay, and resurface only a flat, stable patio. Owners commonly report cracks reappearing on moving slabs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I repair or replace my concrete patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Repair if the slab is flat, solid and the damage is limited to cracks or a worn surface. Replace if more than about a third of it is broken, heaving, sinking unevenly or crumbling, or if you want a different size or layout anyway. Partial replacement of failed sections between control joints is a cheaper middle option.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a patio crack repair last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A polyurethane sealant over backer rod in a clean crack commonly lasts several years before needing touch-up, longer on stable slabs. Acrylic and latex fillers usually wear faster. Resurfacing can last many years on a sound slab with good prep. Leveling lasts as long as the drainage problem that caused the settlement stays fixed.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Resurfacing a moving slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thin overlay bonded to a patio that heaves or settles cracks along the same lines within a season or two. Owners commonly report spending a weekend resurfacing only to see every old crack reappear. Fix movement first by leveling, drainage or replacement, then resurface only a slab that is flat and stable.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling control joints with rigid patch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Control joints exist so the slab cracks where you want it to. Packing them with cement patch or epoxy locks the slab, and it cracks somewhere else instead. If joints need sealing against weeds and water, use a flexible polyurethane sealant over backer rod so the joint can still open and close.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the drainage that caused it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many patio cracks start with a downspout dumping water at the slab edge or a grade that runs water underneath. Repairing the crack without fixing the water means the base keeps washing out. Extend downspouts, correct the slope and seal the joint against the house before or alongside any repair.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Raising the patio too high with pavers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Laying pavers or tile over an existing slab adds 1 to 2 inches or more. That can bring the patio up to the door threshold, create a trip edge at the lawn or send water toward the house. Measure the threshold clearance and slope before buying materials, not after the first course is down.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using rock salt on a repaired patio</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rock salt and ammonium-based deicers drive freeze-thaw damage and scaling, especially on fresh patches and resurfacers. Avoid them for at least the first winter after a repair, and ideally for good. Sand for traction or a concrete-safe deicer protects both the repair and the original slab around it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

